Here's a simple way:

```python
def find_duplicates(items):
    seen = set()
    dupes = set()
    for item in items:
        if item in seen:
            dupes.add(item)
        else:
            seen.add(item)
    return list(dupes)

print(find_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 1]))  # [1, 2]
```

It remembers every item it has seen, and anything that shows up a second time goes into dupes. The order of the result can vary, since sets aren't ordered.

To center a div horizontally, give it a width and set its left and right margins to auto:

```css
.box {
  width: 300px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}
```

Sorry, I only covered half of it! The easiest way to center in both directions is flexbox on the parent element:

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  height: 100vh;
}
```

justify-content centers it horizontally, align-items centers it vertically, and the height gives the parent room to center within. Here it fills the whole screen.

Sure! Here's the classic factorial example:

```python
def factorial(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)

print(factorial(5))  # 120
```

factorial(5) calls factorial(4), which calls factorial(3), and so on down to factorial(1), which just returns 1. Then each call multiplies on the way back up.
